## Independently derived all-fixed-couplings obstruction

Let X0 be a fair sign and let independent transitions flip with probability
pk=1/(k+2). Every coordinate remains fair, but adjacent rates differ, so X is
not stationary. A length r+1 window starting at n is constant exactly when
none of its r adjacent transitions flips. Telescoping gives probability
(n+1)/(n+r+1); its two constant words have equal probabilities. Its total
variation distance from the fair constant-word law is r/(n+r+1), including
r=0. Uniform cylinder approximation on compact binary product space therefore
gives weak convergence of shifted laws to the fair constant-path mixture.
The limit is stationary and nonergodic, which the recovered source permits.

For n>m, conditional on the first m+1 coordinates, the expected sign at n is
rho(m,n)Xm, where rho=m(m+1)/(n(n+1)); m=0 correctly has a zero factor.
For any fixed bounded full-path-measurable g, put gm=E[g|Fm]. The estimate
|E[Xng]| <= ||g-gm||1 + rho(m,n)||gm||1 tends to zero by first letting n
and then m tend to infinity. The coordinate sigma-fields generate the full
path sigma-field; upward conditional-expectation convergence supplies L1
approximation. This is not uniform over functions gn depending on n.

In any fixed joining with the limiting process, that process is almost surely
a constant sign B on all coordinates (a countable intersection). Project B
onto the sigma-field of the whole X path, g=E[B|sigma(X)]. This bounded g can
encode anticipative dependence and extra joint randomness. The tower identity
E[XnB]=E[Xng] and the preceding limit imply P(Xn!=B)->1/2. Conditioning on B
need not preserve independence; this proof never assumes it. A different
coupling for every n is not one fixed joining of the original two processes.

Independently, the marginal two-time mismatch is
(3n+1)/(4(2n+1))->3/8. If Xn approached one fixed B in probability, the union
bound would force this mismatch to vanish. This gives a second mechanism
refuting synchronous convergence even without the stronger half-mismatch limit.

## Metric, offsets and boundary cases

For the specified geometric product metric, let Dn=d(theta_nX,(B,B,...)) and
In=1{Xn!=B}. The pointwise binary triangle inequality and Tonelli give
E|Dn-In| <= sum_j 2^(-j-1)P(X(n+j)!=Xn) <=1/(n+1), since the weighted
index sum is one. Thus Dn tends in law to a fair mixture of0 and1 and its mean
tends to1/2. This numerical limiting law refers to this metric. Uniform
equivalence of compatible metrics on compact binary path space preserves
failure of convergence to zero, not necessarily those same numerical endpoints.

For each deterministic nonnegative offset s, the expected distance between
theta_(n+s)X and theta_nX is at most s/(n+1). For a possibly fully dependent
offset T, on T<=M bound by the sum of all deterministic offsets0,...,M, then
Markov gives a term M(M+1)/(2 epsilon(n+1)) plus P(T>M). First n and then M
tend to infinity. A.s.finite offsets and uniformly tight nonnegative families
are covered without conditioning on T. Arbitrarily escaping offsets are not.
The second process is constant, so shifts of it have no effect.

Independent divergent flip probabilities make eventual constancy have X
probability0 and limit probability1. This Borel shift-invariant event rules
out setwise convergence and prevents any refutation of distinct Problem3.1.
Audit-only growing first-hit offsets and the probability family's separate iid
duplicate-block route are boundary evidence, not an expanded original theorem,
new submitted discovery or author attempt. The exact target gap remains an
alternative general two-process characterization.

